1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,800
As David said, I've been at this for a long time.

2
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,180
I am not a medical doctor, but I've probably learned enough medicine to be able to play

3
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:29,640
one on television over the years.

4
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:34,360
And actually, that's relevant to today's lecture, because today's lecture is really

5
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:40,260
trying to set the scene for you to say, well, what are the kinds of problems that doctors

6
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:45,960
are interested in by looking at what is it that they do?

7
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:49,200
So that's our goal for today.

8
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,920
So what we're going to do today is to talk about, in a very general way, what are the

9
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:55,720
goals of healthcare?

10
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:59,480
So how many of you are doctors?

11
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:00,480
A couple.

12
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:01,480
Great.

13
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:02,480
Okay.

14
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,040
So fix me when I blow it, all right?

15
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:09,320
Please feel free to interrupt.

16
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:11,600
So that's going to be my first task.

17
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,560
And then the second one is going to be, what are the things that people actually do in

18
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:21,160
order to try to achieve these goals?

19
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:24,560
What is the practice of medicine like?

20
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:30,920
What is the process that generates the data that we're going to be using to learn from?

21
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:36,120
And then I can't resist talking a little bit about paying for healthcare at the end of

22
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:41,800
the lecture, because a lot of the problems that come up and a lot of the interest that

23
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:47,080
people show in doing the kind of analysis we're talking about, in fact, is motivated

24
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:48,240
by money.

25
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:52,680
They want to be able to save money or spend less money or something like that.

26
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:55,040
So it's important to know that.

27
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,600
Okay.

28
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:58,440
Medicine's been around for a long time.

29
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:06,840
I think from probably the earliest of recorded history, there are discussions of people wondering

30
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:11,600
what the cause of disease is, how to cure it.

31
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:17,840
They came up with some fairly cockamamie theories, because they didn't have a lot of scientific

32
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:20,760
modern approaches to it.

33
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:27,720
But for example, this is a photo on the left of a shaman, I think, from a Canadian Indian

34
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:33,240
tribe who's working on the boy lying there who's sick.

35
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:40,800
And this shaman would use his knowledge of experience that he'd had with other patients.

36
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,120
They did know a lot about medicinal plants.

37
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:50,160
They knew something about how to care for injuries and things like that.

38
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:53,560
And so this was an effective form of health care.

39
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:54,840
Not much record keeping.

40
00:02:54,840 --> 00:03:01,000
You don't see an electronic medical record system in that scene.

41
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,960
On the right is a modern shaman practicing at one of the New York area hospitals.

42
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:13,920
And so there are traditional cultures in which that sort of hands-on interaction with the

43
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:19,560
healer is considered a very important part of the practice of medicine.

44
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:26,360
And if you listen to futurists, doctors, talking about what medicine is likely to be like,

45
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:34,160
they emphasize the fact that the role of a healer is not just to be a good automaton

46
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:41,280
who figures out the right things, but is to persuade a patient to trust him to do the

47
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:46,080
things that he or she is suggesting to the patient.

48
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:50,840
And there are a lot of placebo effects that we know from lots and lots of experiments

49
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:55,920
that say that if you think you're going to get better, you are going to get better on

50
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:59,320
average, no guarantees.

51
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:04,480
OK, now modern medicine actually looks more like this.

52
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:07,000
So this is an intensive care unit.

53
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,400
And what you see is a patient who has got all kinds of electrical leads and tubes and

54
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:22,520
things going into him, and is surrounded by tons and tons of equipment, which is monitoring

55
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:26,040
him and perhaps keeping him alive.

56
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:31,800
And so this is the high-tech medicine that we think of as the contemporary version of

57
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,200
clinical care.

58
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:41,960
Well, you might say, OK, what does it mean to be healthy, right?

59
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:48,520
If the goal of medicine is to make people or keep people healthy, what is health?

60
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:53,680
So we turn to the World Health Organization, and they have this lovely, very comprehensive

61
00:04:53,680 --> 00:05:00,280
notion of the definition of health, a state of complete physical, mental, and social well-being,

62
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:03,480
not merely the absence of disease or infirmity.

63
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:07,840
And then they categorize this, and they say, well, there's physical health, there's mental

64
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,680
health, and there's social health.

65
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:15,800
Social health is especially hard to measure, and I'll come back to that in a little while.

66
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:20,440
So what's easiest to measure is how long people live.

67
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:29,080
And so we've had data on survival analysis for a long time, and this is kind of shocking.

68
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:37,760
If you look here, this lower curve is from around 1800, and what it shows you is that

69
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:45,680
if you lived in India around 1800, your life expectancy was about 25 years.

70
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:48,240
It's not very good.

71
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:54,440
And if you lived in the richest countries, which in those days were typically European,

72
00:05:54,440 --> 00:06:00,560
in Belgium, your life expectancy was way up there at 40 years.

73
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,760
How many of you knew that?

74
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:04,560
OK, good.

75
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:08,160
I didn't until I started looking at this.

76
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:17,960
Now by 1950, which is not that long ago, it was like 69 years ago, in Norway, your expectation

77
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:22,760
was that you'd live into your early 70s and in the US that you would live into your late

78
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:25,880
60s on average.

79
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:33,280
There was still a huge cliff where if you lived in Bhutan or Somalia or something, you

80
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:38,000
were still down around 30.

81
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,600
Today, well, in 2012, we're doing a lot better.

82
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:48,720
And the thing that's striking is not only that the people who were doing well have gotten

83
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:54,440
better, but that a lot of the people who were doing very poorly have also gotten better.

84
00:06:54,440 --> 00:07:00,720
And so we're now up, you know, India, remember, was at 25 years of life expectancy, and now

85
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:02,840
it's in the high 60s.

86
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:08,280
And now, of course, these are averages, and so individuals vary a lot, but that's kind

87
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:09,280
of interesting.

88
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:17,000
So if you look at the numbers, you see that even on a shorter term, there are big changes.

89
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:25,400
So for example, if you're a male living in Rwanda, which is among the worst places in

90
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:31,800
terms of life expectancy, your life expectancy, if you're born today, is about 62 and a half

91
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,600
years.

92
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:39,040
If you were born in 2001, it was only 38 years.

93
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:43,160
Now what was going on in Rwanda in 2001?

94
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:47,900
Genocide, yeah, they were killing each other.

95
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:52,400
So that's sort of an exceptional situation.

96
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:58,280
And that's gotten much better because they've stopped killing each other as genocidal attacks

97
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:04,040
between the Hutu and the Tutsi, I think, if I remember right.

98
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:05,320
What about South Africa?

99
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:15,640
What was going on in South Africa in 2001?

100
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:17,120
I'm not sure I heard you.

101
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:19,360
Failure to address the HIV crisis.

102
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:27,600
Yes, the government at the time was claiming that HIV was not the cause of AIDS, and therefore

103
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:34,280
there was no point in controlling HIV infections because AIDS was caused by something else.

104
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:36,280
Pretty crazy.

105
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:40,000
So that was terrible, and they've gotten much better at it.

106
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,920
So that's what you tend to see in a lot of African countries.

107
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:50,040
And what you also see is that there has really been improvement everywhere.

108
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:58,300
So in the US, we went from males expected to live 74 years to almost 78 years.

109
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:05,680
So about a four-year increase in life expectancy over a period of just 17 years.

110
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:13,000
You women, by the way, are going to outlive us men on average.

111
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,720
There's some biological thing that seems to work that way.

112
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:37,000
OK, so a typical way that people look at survival of a population is to say, well, given a cohort of people born at some instant zero, what fraction of them are still alive after a certain period of time?

113
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:42,240
And what you see is that, of course, 2031, we haven't reached yet.

114
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:51,280
And so these are projections based on theoretical extrapolations of actual data, but the older data is real.

115
00:09:51,280 --> 00:10:02,360
And what you see is that from 1851 to 2011, let's say, these numbers have gone way up.

116
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:04,640
Now, where have they gone up the most?

117
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:11,320
Well, it used to be that childhood mortality was enormous.

118
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:19,840
And so if you look at 1851, by age 10, about 30% of children had died.

119
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:25,800
And so we've gotten a lot better at stopping that from happening.

120
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:30,160
People also look at curves like this.

121
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:34,440
So this is a distribution of death rates by age.

122
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:37,600
This happens to be for Japan a few years ago.

123
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:40,640
And again, females do better than males.

124
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:45,720
The gold curve in the middle is the average of the two.

125
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:50,760
And this is very typical of almost any country that you look at.

126
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:55,280
The shape of this curve is pretty universal.

127
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:56,920
So what does this say?

128
00:10:56,920 --> 00:11:03,680
It says that when you're born, there's a relatively high risk that you're going to die.

129
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:07,840
So these are kids who have congenital abnormalities,

130
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:14,840
have prenatal problems, have all kinds of difficulties, and they don't make it.

131
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:19,360
So there's a fairly high death rate at birth.

132
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:24,200
But once you make it to about, I think, about two years old,

133
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:32,720
the death rate is down to about 1 in 10,000 per year.

134
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:38,840
And then it stays quite low until you become a teenager.

135
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:42,200
Now, why might the death rate go up when you become a teenager?

136
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:48,400
Well, suicide is the extreme example of that.

137
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:54,520
But teenagers tend to be risk seeking rather than risk averse.

138
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:56,080
They start driving cars.

139
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:01,960
They go skiing, skydiving, whatever it is that they're doing.

140
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,080
And they start dying.

141
00:12:04,080 --> 00:12:10,760
But then if you make it to about 20, then there's a relatively flat region,

142
00:12:10,760 --> 00:12:15,240
whereby then you've developed enough sense to know what risks are worth taking

143
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:17,280
and which ones aren't.

144
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:23,240
And so it's relatively flat until about age 35 or 40,

145
00:12:23,240 --> 00:12:28,280
at which point it starts inexorably rising.

146
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:30,720
And of course, as you get older and older,

147
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:33,480
the probability that you're going to die in the next year

148
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:35,960
becomes higher and higher.

149
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:41,320
This is uncomfortable for somebody with my amount of gray hair.

150
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:44,280
Now, there is a peculiarity in Japan

151
00:12:44,280 --> 00:12:46,480
which people puzzled over for a while,

152
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:52,160
and that is that weird dip up at age 106.

153
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:55,840
So first of all, that's a very small number of people

154
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:58,000
that that's represents.

155
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,160
And it turned out that it was fraud.

156
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:05,560
So there were families who failed to report

157
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:10,360
the death of their ancient grandmother or great-grandmother

158
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:14,480
because they wanted to continue collecting social security payments

159
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:15,440
from the government.

160
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:17,480
So that's an artifact.

161
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:18,980
OK.

162
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:27,600
Now, this is a serious problem which we're

163
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:31,920
going to return to in a more technical way later in the semester,

164
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:34,440
which is this problem of disparities.

165
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:37,560
So if you look at, for example, the difference

166
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:43,720
between white and black female life expectancy in the United States,

167
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:47,080
you see that everybody's life expectancy, as we've shown,

168
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:53,200
is going up gradually, in this case, from 1975 to 2015.

169
00:13:53,200 --> 00:13:57,800
But there continues to be a gap between black and white females

170
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:00,600
and between black and white males,

171
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:05,920
where black patients are more likely to die

172
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:08,800
or less likely to survive longer,

173
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:15,200
given the disparities that exist socioeconomically, maybe

174
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:18,600
medically, we don't know exactly.

175
00:14:18,600 --> 00:14:23,680
And then if you look at Hispanics, however, they do pretty well.

176
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:27,640
So in 2015, you're actually a little bit better off

177
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:30,000
to be Hispanic, either male or female,

178
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,680
than you are to be either white or black.

179
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:37,520
But it's still worse to be black than to be white or Hispanic.

180
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:43,240
So these are the kinds of facts that drive some of the issues

181
00:14:43,240 --> 00:14:46,080
in what we do in medical care.

182
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:48,160
Now, what do people die of?

183
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:52,000
Well, about a quarter of them die of heart disease

184
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,520
and a little over a fifth of them die of cancer.

185
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:59,120
This is USA data from 2014, so it's not completely up to date,

186
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:01,880
but hasn't changed that much.

187
00:15:01,880 --> 00:15:05,640
And then there's a decreasing number of deaths

188
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:08,200
from various other causes.

189
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:11,720
So heart disease, cancer, chronic lower respiratory disease.

190
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:18,200
So this is like COPD that's caused by smoking, stuff like that.

191
00:15:18,200 --> 00:15:23,320
Accidents account for about 5% of deaths, stroke,

192
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:28,080
cerebral vascular events, Alzheimer's disease, diabetes,

193
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:31,760
influenza, pneumonia, kidney disease, suicide,

194
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:36,320
and then everything else is about another quarter.

195
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:38,440
Now, take a look at those.

196
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:41,640
What kind of diseases are these?

197
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:42,320
The biggies.

198
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:48,800
Well, they're chronic.

199
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:51,960
Most of them are chronic.

200
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:58,000
They're also not infectious, except for influenza and pneumonia.

201
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,720
Nothing else there is infectious, as far as we know.

202
00:16:05,800 --> 00:16:10,440
That asterisk you should put after every statement

203
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:13,080
about current medicine.

204
00:16:13,080 --> 00:16:17,200
So that's interesting, because if you wrote the same table back

205
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:20,800
in 1850, you would find that a lot of people

206
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:22,880
were dying of infections.

207
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:25,680
And they weren't typically living long enough

208
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:30,560
to develop these lovely chronic diseases of the aging.

209
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:35,120
So there have been big changes there.

210
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:37,920
Now, the other thing that's worth looking at

211
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:42,160
is, in addition to the reasons that people die,

212
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:43,880
they start getting sicker.

213
00:16:43,880 --> 00:16:48,840
And they are getting sort of less value out of life

214
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:52,680
because they're developing all these other conditions.

215
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:58,320
So if you look at people over 65, about half of them

216
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:03,000
have arthritis, some form of arthritis.

217
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:09,360
And about 40% have hypertension.

218
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:13,760
By the way, if you have trouble with any of the medicalese

219
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:15,840
words, just interrupt.

220
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:18,600
Hypertension is high blood pressure.

221
00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:21,280
Hearing impairment.

222
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:25,120
Me, I'm wearing a hearing aid on one side

223
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:28,520
because my ears are going bad.

224
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:32,160
Heart disease, about a quarter, orthostatic impairment.

225
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:36,160
That means people who wobble because their sense of balance

226
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:37,440
is not so good.

227
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:43,520
16%, cataracts, chronic sinusitis, visual impairment,

228
00:17:43,520 --> 00:17:48,320
genitourinary problems, diabetes, et cetera.

229
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:50,360
So these are all growing.

230
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:53,400
Here's the list of the next 10.

231
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:58,880
And varicose veins, hernia, hemorrhoids, psoriasis,

232
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:01,320
hardening of the arteries, tinnitus,

233
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:04,480
corns, calluses, constipation, hay fever,

234
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:07,440
and cerebrovascular problems.

235
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:13,520
So people develop these by the time they're over 65.

236
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:16,240
So one question we might ask is, well,

237
00:18:16,240 --> 00:18:18,680
what is the quality of life?

238
00:18:18,680 --> 00:18:22,760
So for example, a lot of the doctors

239
00:18:22,760 --> 00:18:26,280
that I started working with in the 1970s

240
00:18:26,280 --> 00:18:30,200
were great advocates of the application of decision

241
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:34,760
analysis, decision theory, to making medical decisions.

242
00:18:34,760 --> 00:18:39,240
And so the problem is, how do you evaluate an outcome?

243
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:42,560
And they said, well, the way we evaluate an outcome

244
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:45,320
is we look at your longevity.

245
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:49,360
Obviously, the longer you live, the better, typically.

246
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:53,400
But we also look at your quality of life during that time.

247
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:58,720
And we say that if you're confined to a wheelchair,

248
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:01,320
let's say your quality of life might not

249
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:05,520
be as good as if you were able to run around.

250
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:08,400
Or if you're suffering from chronic pain,

251
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:10,680
your quality of life might not be as good

252
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:13,800
as if you were pain-free.

253
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:17,800
And so we came up with this model that says, well,

254
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:22,280
the value of your life is essentially

255
00:19:22,280 --> 00:19:26,760
an integral from time 0 to however long you're

256
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:31,320
going to live of a function q that says,

257
00:19:31,320 --> 00:19:34,960
this is a measure of how good your quality of life

258
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:38,200
is at that particular point in time.

259
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:41,120
And then some discount factor.

260
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:43,800
So what's the role of the discount factor?

261
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:45,840
Well, it's just like in economics.

262
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:52,720
If I offer you some horribly painful thing today versus 10

263
00:19:52,720 --> 00:19:56,400
years from now, which are you going to choose?

264
00:19:56,400 --> 00:20:00,240
Most of us will say later.

265
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:02,960
So that's what the discount factor does.

266
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:06,800
Now, who knows what the right discount rate is.

267
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:09,640
So in some of their work, they were doing crazy things

268
00:20:09,640 --> 00:20:12,120
like taking the financial discount

269
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:15,160
factors about bank interest rates

270
00:20:15,160 --> 00:20:18,360
and things like that and applying them to these health

271
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:21,680
things just because they didn't have any better numbers to do.

272
00:20:21,680 --> 00:20:23,400
That seems a little suspicious.

273
00:20:23,400 --> 00:20:25,240
But nevertheless, methodologically,

274
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:27,480
it's a way of doing it.

275
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:30,920
OK, so how do you measure the quality of life?

276
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:35,560
Well, there's this notion of the activities of daily living.

277
00:20:35,560 --> 00:20:38,000
So can you bathe and shower?

278
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:41,960
Can you brush your teeth and groom your hair?

279
00:20:41,960 --> 00:20:42,920
Can you get dressed?

280
00:20:42,960 --> 00:20:46,200
Can you go to the toilet, clean yourself up?

281
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:49,320
Are you able to walk, get in and out of bed,

282
00:20:49,320 --> 00:20:51,360
get in and out of a chair?

283
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:53,440
Can you feed yourself?

284
00:20:53,440 --> 00:20:56,480
And then there are a bunch of instrumental factors,

285
00:20:56,480 --> 00:20:59,760
things like, are you able to clean your house?

286
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:02,840
And can you manage your money, and so on.

287
00:21:02,840 --> 00:21:06,200
So these are typically for older people.

288
00:21:06,200 --> 00:21:10,480
But they are ways of trying to quantify that quality of life

289
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:14,960
by saying, how many of these things are you able to do?

290
00:21:14,960 --> 00:21:18,200
And there are a lot of federal regulations, for example,

291
00:21:18,200 --> 00:21:22,240
that take advantage of quantification like this.

292
00:21:22,240 --> 00:21:26,760
So if you're asking to be put on some sort of disability

293
00:21:26,760 --> 00:21:30,920
where the government sends you a check to keep you alive,

294
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:34,520
you have to demonstrate that you are at a certain point

295
00:21:34,520 --> 00:21:38,680
on the scale that's derived from these capabilities in order

296
00:21:38,680 --> 00:21:41,880
to justifiably get that.

297
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:45,240
So occupational therapy is one of the things

298
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:49,320
that people try to teach the elderly.

299
00:21:49,320 --> 00:21:54,280
My parents died in their late 80s, and my dad was 90.

300
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:59,200
And I remember when he would have some medical problem,

301
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:01,400
then he would be put into the clutches

302
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:03,840
of an occupational therapist who would

303
00:22:03,840 --> 00:22:10,400
try to make sure that he was able to communicate and get

304
00:22:10,400 --> 00:22:15,560
around and not fall for tricks where people wanted

305
00:22:15,560 --> 00:22:19,000
to get him to send all his money to them,

306
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:22,840
or the very meal preparation and stuff like that.

307
00:22:22,840 --> 00:22:28,720
So these are sort of the, occupational is a funny term

308
00:22:28,720 --> 00:22:31,720
for it, because this typically applies to people

309
00:22:31,720 --> 00:22:35,200
who are retired, so it's not really an occupation.

310
00:22:35,200 --> 00:22:36,880
But it's the sort of things that you

311
00:22:36,880 --> 00:22:41,000
need to do in order to be able to have a decent life.

312
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:44,480
Well, now there's an interesting valuation question.

313
00:22:44,480 --> 00:22:48,760
So if you look at the top right model,

314
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:52,880
we actually don't have very good data on anything

315
00:22:52,880 --> 00:22:54,240
other than mortality.

316
00:22:54,240 --> 00:22:56,480
So mortality is who dies.

317
00:22:56,480 --> 00:22:59,840
So the blue curve there is the curve

318
00:22:59,840 --> 00:23:02,880
that you've seen before, which is of a cohort,

319
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:07,240
how many people are still alive after a certain number of years.

320
00:23:07,240 --> 00:23:11,720
The red curve is a morbidity curve,

321
00:23:11,720 --> 00:23:16,560
which says how many of those people are still alive

322
00:23:16,560 --> 00:23:22,800
and have no sort of problematic chronic diseases.

323
00:23:22,800 --> 00:23:27,080
So they're not in constant pain, and they're not immobilized,

324
00:23:27,080 --> 00:23:31,680
and they're not unable to do the things that I just

325
00:23:31,680 --> 00:23:35,480
listed on the previous slides.

326
00:23:35,480 --> 00:23:39,880
And then disability is when you really

327
00:23:39,880 --> 00:23:43,480
become incapable of taking care of yourself,

328
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:47,120
and it typically involves moving into an assisted living

329
00:23:47,120 --> 00:23:49,720
facility or a nursing home or something

330
00:23:49,720 --> 00:23:53,760
like that, which is kind of a nightmare for many people,

331
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:56,680
and also very expensive for society.

332
00:23:56,680 --> 00:24:00,280
So as I said, the blue curve there

333
00:24:00,280 --> 00:24:05,960
is based on actual data for American females in 1980.

334
00:24:05,960 --> 00:24:09,360
The red curve is a hypothetical curve,

335
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:14,760
where I just assumed that the rate of developing a morbidity

336
00:24:14,760 --> 00:24:18,200
is about twice the rate of dying.

337
00:24:18,200 --> 00:24:22,040
And the green curve, I assumed that the rate

338
00:24:22,080 --> 00:24:28,080
of developing a disability is, I can't remember,

339
00:24:28,080 --> 00:24:31,600
I think, three times as high as the rate of dying or something

340
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:32,120
like that.

341
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:35,320
So that's why those curves are lower,

342
00:24:35,320 --> 00:24:37,240
and they look approximately right,

343
00:24:37,240 --> 00:24:40,320
but we don't have good data on those.

344
00:24:40,320 --> 00:24:43,400
Now, the question that you have to ask

345
00:24:43,400 --> 00:24:46,160
is, how do you want to change this?

346
00:24:46,160 --> 00:24:51,000
So for example, suppose that we kept the same situation,

347
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:56,280
we reduced mortality to 20% of its actual rate,

348
00:24:56,280 --> 00:25:00,840
but we kept the disability and morbidity rates the same

349
00:25:00,840 --> 00:25:03,360
as they are on the top left.

350
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:04,600
So what would that do?

351
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:07,080
Well, what that would do is create

352
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:13,960
a huge number of people who are disabled, right?

353
00:25:14,960 --> 00:25:19,920
Because they're going to live longer beyond the point where

354
00:25:19,920 --> 00:25:22,720
they're able to function fully.

355
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:24,720
So this is not, yeah.

356
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:27,720
Can I just ask, why is green not just mean healthy?

357
00:25:27,720 --> 00:25:28,720
Like to me, it just seems healthy.

358
00:25:28,720 --> 00:25:30,720
Green just means healthy.

359
00:25:30,720 --> 00:25:31,720
Oh, OK.

360
00:25:31,720 --> 00:25:32,720
It does.

361
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:33,720
Yeah.

362
00:25:33,720 --> 00:25:36,720
So beyond green is what I meant is the morbidity curve,

363
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:39,720
and beyond red is the disability curve.

364
00:25:39,720 --> 00:25:40,720
OK.

365
00:25:40,720 --> 00:25:41,720
I may have misspoken.

366
00:25:41,720 --> 00:25:42,720
No, it's fine.

367
00:25:43,480 --> 00:25:44,480
All right.

368
00:25:44,480 --> 00:25:47,480
So green is healthy.

369
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:52,040
Red means suffering from some morbidity,

370
00:25:52,040 --> 00:25:54,680
and blue means disabled.

371
00:25:54,680 --> 00:25:55,520
OK.

372
00:25:55,520 --> 00:25:59,880
So if we just extend life, but we don't make it any better,

373
00:25:59,880 --> 00:26:03,320
then that's not a very attractive picture.

374
00:26:03,320 --> 00:26:06,560
So other possibilities are compression of morbidity.

375
00:26:06,720 --> 00:26:14,360
So for example, if we reduce the rate at which people

376
00:26:14,360 --> 00:26:21,920
get sick and die, but we decrease it initially

377
00:26:21,920 --> 00:26:25,600
and then increase it so that, on average, people

378
00:26:25,600 --> 00:26:28,880
live about the same length of time that they now do,

379
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:32,360
then we're going to have fewer people who are suffering

380
00:26:32,360 --> 00:26:35,760
from morbidity or who are disabled.

381
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:41,400
Because you last doing well, and then you die.

382
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:45,000
So this is the wonderful one horseshoe,

383
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:50,400
where everything falls apart at once, which, frankly,

384
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:52,840
as somebody who's a little closer to the end

385
00:26:52,840 --> 00:26:57,840
than you guys are, I wouldn't mind that kind of exit.

386
00:26:57,840 --> 00:27:01,640
I don't want to be disabled for 20 years.

387
00:27:01,640 --> 00:27:03,600
I'd rather live a long time healthy

388
00:27:03,600 --> 00:27:05,920
and then drop that at some point.

389
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:06,400
OK.

390
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:12,000
My dad used to say that he wanted

391
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:15,800
to die by being hit by a meteor.

392
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:17,560
He wouldn't know it's coming.

393
00:27:17,560 --> 00:27:20,440
It's instant, no suffering, no pain.

394
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:21,600
Perfect.

395
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:25,840
He almost got it, but not quite.

396
00:27:25,840 --> 00:27:26,520
OK.

397
00:27:26,520 --> 00:27:30,640
And then the final story is lifespan extension,

398
00:27:30,680 --> 00:27:38,240
which is where we simply lower the mortality rate

399
00:27:38,240 --> 00:27:40,800
and all the other rates in proportion.

400
00:27:40,800 --> 00:27:42,640
And what happens is that you start

401
00:27:42,640 --> 00:27:50,920
having healthy 107-year-olds and not so healthy 120-year-olds

402
00:27:50,920 --> 00:27:55,840
in the population in larger numbers than we do now.

403
00:27:55,840 --> 00:27:57,400
OK.

404
00:27:57,400 --> 00:27:59,200
Social quality of life.

405
00:27:59,200 --> 00:28:02,360
That's a tough one, right?

406
00:28:02,360 --> 00:28:05,320
So here's a naive theory.

407
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:08,960
We take the quality of life of every person on the planet,

408
00:28:08,960 --> 00:28:11,760
we sum them up, and we say, OK, that's

409
00:28:11,760 --> 00:28:13,280
the social quality of life.

410
00:28:16,120 --> 00:28:19,000
Is that a good idea?

411
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:20,720
Probably not, right?

412
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:22,480
For one thing, it would say that we

413
00:28:22,480 --> 00:28:26,800
ought to have as much population explosion as possible,

414
00:28:26,800 --> 00:28:30,400
because then we have more people to integrate over,

415
00:28:30,400 --> 00:28:34,760
which doesn't seem sensible, right?

416
00:28:34,760 --> 00:28:38,280
Now, obviously, if we start packing the world

417
00:28:38,280 --> 00:28:41,960
so that it's super crowded, then the quality of life

418
00:28:41,960 --> 00:28:45,760
will go down eventually enough that adding more people

419
00:28:45,760 --> 00:28:48,160
is probably not optimal.

420
00:28:48,160 --> 00:28:49,960
But nevertheless, that doesn't seem

421
00:28:49,960 --> 00:28:56,720
like a real satisfying solution.

422
00:28:56,720 --> 00:28:58,400
How about less?

423
00:28:58,400 --> 00:29:00,880
It was popular about 10 years ago

424
00:29:00,880 --> 00:29:05,000
for people to write these speculative books about how

425
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:09,680
would the world look if half the people died, right?

426
00:29:09,680 --> 00:29:13,960
Other than the trauma of the half the people dying,

427
00:29:13,960 --> 00:29:15,920
they were proposing that this would

428
00:29:15,920 --> 00:29:25,440
be some wonderful Sylvan ideal, old-fashioned kind of world.

429
00:29:25,440 --> 00:29:27,040
I didn't buy that.

430
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:30,760
And of course, we don't know of a good way to get there.

431
00:29:30,760 --> 00:29:34,560
Although it is true that in at least developed countries,

432
00:29:34,560 --> 00:29:37,400
birth rates keep falling to the point

433
00:29:37,400 --> 00:29:41,920
where populations are worried about underpopulation.

434
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:46,020
The Japanese, for example, have very strict immigration

435
00:29:46,020 --> 00:29:48,840
policies, so you can't become Japanese

436
00:29:48,840 --> 00:29:51,240
if you're not born Japanese.

437
00:29:51,240 --> 00:29:53,840
And Japanese people aren't having enough kids

438
00:29:53,840 --> 00:29:55,560
to replace themselves.

439
00:29:55,560 --> 00:30:01,160
And so the natural population of Japan is falling.

440
00:30:01,160 --> 00:30:05,000
Italy is in the same quandary, except that Italy

441
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,640
has all these immigrants coming in and trying

442
00:30:08,640 --> 00:30:09,880
to become Italians.

443
00:30:09,880 --> 00:30:12,320
And of course, this leads to big political fights

444
00:30:12,320 --> 00:30:18,080
about who gets to be an Italian and who doesn't.

445
00:30:18,080 --> 00:30:20,640
And then, of course, there's the question of money, which,

446
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:23,400
as I say, we'll return to later.

447
00:30:23,400 --> 00:30:26,800
Now, one other important thing to consider

448
00:30:26,800 --> 00:30:32,880
is that because of the increase in life expectancy,

449
00:30:32,880 --> 00:30:36,040
there's been a big change in timescale,

450
00:30:36,040 --> 00:30:40,120
in the way people think about medical care.

451
00:30:40,120 --> 00:30:46,040
So it used to be, long, long ago, in the shaman era,

452
00:30:46,040 --> 00:30:49,600
you wouldn't go to a shaman to say, keep me healthy.

453
00:30:49,600 --> 00:30:53,160
You would go to a shaman saying, I broke my arm,

454
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:58,320
or I have this pain in my leg, or fix me somehow.

455
00:30:58,320 --> 00:31:03,080
And so things were focused on the notion of cure.

456
00:31:03,080 --> 00:31:07,000
And that was applicable to acute illnesses.

457
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:11,080
But as we've gotten better at treating acute illnesses,

458
00:31:11,080 --> 00:31:13,960
which, by the way, didn't happen all that long ago.

459
00:31:13,960 --> 00:31:16,440
I mean, antibiotics were only invented

460
00:31:16,440 --> 00:31:18,480
in the early 20th century.

461
00:31:18,480 --> 00:31:22,080
And that made a huge difference in stopping people

462
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:24,800
from dying of infections.

463
00:31:24,800 --> 00:31:27,560
So then it became more a matter of managing

464
00:31:27,560 --> 00:31:30,000
one-term chronic illnesses.

465
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,120
And that's pretty much where we are now.

466
00:31:32,120 --> 00:31:36,360
The medical world at the moment, most of the action

467
00:31:36,360 --> 00:31:39,240
is in trying to understand things like diabetes,

468
00:31:39,240 --> 00:31:41,880
and heart disease, and cancer, and things

469
00:31:41,880 --> 00:31:44,720
like that that develop over a long time.

470
00:31:44,720 --> 00:31:46,520
And they don't kill you instantly,

471
00:31:46,520 --> 00:31:49,640
like infectious diseases did.

472
00:31:49,640 --> 00:31:53,360
But they produce a real burden.

473
00:31:53,360 --> 00:31:56,840
And then, of course, the next step that everybody expects

474
00:31:56,840 --> 00:32:00,240
is, well, how do we prevent disease?

475
00:32:00,240 --> 00:32:03,720
So how can we change your exposures?

476
00:32:03,720 --> 00:32:05,520
How can we change your motivation?

477
00:32:05,520 --> 00:32:06,960
How can we change your diet?

478
00:32:06,960 --> 00:32:10,640
How can we change whatever it is that we need to change?

479
00:32:10,640 --> 00:32:13,680
How can we change your genes to prevent you

480
00:32:13,680 --> 00:32:16,840
from developing these diseases in the first place?

481
00:32:16,840 --> 00:32:19,520
And that's sort of the future.

482
00:32:19,520 --> 00:32:25,480
OK, so that's about what medicine tries to do.

483
00:32:25,480 --> 00:32:26,880
But how does it do it?

484
00:32:26,880 --> 00:32:28,880
And so we're going to talk a little bit

485
00:32:28,880 --> 00:32:32,360
about the traditional tasks that are attributed

486
00:32:32,360 --> 00:32:34,080
to health care practice.

487
00:32:34,080 --> 00:32:38,240
So traditionally, people talk about diagnosis, prognosis,

488
00:32:38,240 --> 00:32:39,720
and therapy.

489
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:42,440
So diagnosis, I go to my doctor.

490
00:32:42,440 --> 00:32:45,240
I say, doc, I got this horrible headache.

491
00:32:45,240 --> 00:32:47,480
I've had it for two weeks.

492
00:32:47,480 --> 00:32:50,360
What's wrong with me?

493
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:56,720
And his job, in my case, it happens to be a he.

494
00:32:56,720 --> 00:33:00,640
His job is to come up with an answer.

495
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:01,480
What's wrong with me?

496
00:33:04,400 --> 00:33:08,360
Prognosis is he's supposed to predict

497
00:33:08,360 --> 00:33:11,120
what's going to happen to me, at least

498
00:33:11,120 --> 00:33:14,160
if he doesn't do anything.

499
00:33:14,160 --> 00:33:16,800
So is this headache going to go away?

500
00:33:16,800 --> 00:33:21,240
Or is it going to turn out to be a brain tumor that will kill me?

501
00:33:21,240 --> 00:33:26,320
Or is it going to be some amoeba that's living in my brain

502
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:32,760
and eat my neurons or all kinds of horrible things

503
00:33:32,760 --> 00:33:34,560
or possible?

504
00:33:34,560 --> 00:33:36,400
So it's the prospect of recovery,

505
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:39,760
as anticipated from the usual course of disease

506
00:33:39,760 --> 00:33:42,480
or peculiarities of the case.

507
00:33:42,480 --> 00:33:46,600
And then therapy, of course, is what do you do about it?

508
00:33:46,600 --> 00:33:53,440
And prognosis is definitely informed by diagnosis,

509
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:56,600
because if you don't know what's wrong with me,

510
00:33:56,600 --> 00:34:00,520
then it's much harder to predict what's going to happen to me.

511
00:34:00,520 --> 00:34:03,680
And if you can't predict what's going to happen to me,

512
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:05,720
then it's much harder to figure out

513
00:34:05,720 --> 00:34:08,480
what to do to prevent that from happening

514
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:11,680
or to encourage that to happen.

515
00:34:11,680 --> 00:34:15,600
So this is kind of a serial process.

516
00:34:15,600 --> 00:34:18,560
And the way I look at it is that there's

517
00:34:18,560 --> 00:34:23,160
a kind of cyclic process of care.

518
00:34:23,160 --> 00:34:27,360
And the process starts with an initial presentation.

519
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:29,560
So I show up at my doctor's office

520
00:34:29,560 --> 00:34:31,960
and I complain about something.

521
00:34:31,960 --> 00:34:37,000
And if you ever listen to a doctor interacting

522
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:40,000
with a patient, the first time the patient comes in,

523
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:44,360
the first question is always, what brought you here?

524
00:34:44,360 --> 00:34:47,520
And that's called the presenting complaint.

525
00:34:47,520 --> 00:34:53,680
So if I say my ankle hurts like hell, that's very different

526
00:34:53,680 --> 00:34:58,360
than if I say I stopped being able to hear in my right ear

527
00:34:58,360 --> 00:35:02,440
or I have this horrible skin rash on my arm or whatever.

528
00:35:02,440 --> 00:35:05,880
And that's going to take me in very different directions.

529
00:35:05,880 --> 00:35:10,040
So then what happens is that the doctor examines you

530
00:35:10,040 --> 00:35:12,600
and generates a bunch of data.

531
00:35:12,600 --> 00:35:13,760
So these are measurements.

532
00:35:13,760 --> 00:35:16,840
And of course, it used to be that those measurements were

533
00:35:16,840 --> 00:35:18,880
based on observation.

534
00:35:18,880 --> 00:35:22,480
So there are very famous doctors from 100 years ago

535
00:35:22,480 --> 00:35:25,080
who were spectacularly good at being

536
00:35:25,080 --> 00:35:28,160
able to look at a patient and figure out what's

537
00:35:28,160 --> 00:35:32,080
wrong with them by being various student observers.

538
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:36,400
The Sherlock Holmes kind of subtle, oh,

539
00:35:36,400 --> 00:35:40,120
I see that you have a cut on the inside of your shoe, which

540
00:35:40,120 --> 00:35:43,080
means you must have been going through brambles

541
00:35:43,080 --> 00:35:48,080
and whatever, I'm making up a Sherlock Holmes story here.

542
00:35:48,080 --> 00:35:50,320
So that generates data.

543
00:35:50,320 --> 00:35:53,160
And then we interpret that data to generate

544
00:35:53,160 --> 00:35:59,000
some kind of information or interpreted data about the patient.

545
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:02,760
And based on that, we determine a diagnosis.

546
00:36:02,760 --> 00:36:05,080
Now, do we determine a diagnosis?

547
00:36:05,080 --> 00:36:05,840
Maybe not.

548
00:36:05,840 --> 00:36:08,920
Maybe we guess a diagnosis.

549
00:36:08,920 --> 00:36:12,560
One of the things I learned early on working in this field

550
00:36:12,560 --> 00:36:17,040
is that doctors are actually quite willing to make guesses

551
00:36:17,040 --> 00:36:20,640
because it's so useful to believe that you understand

552
00:36:20,640 --> 00:36:22,560
what's going on.

553
00:36:22,560 --> 00:36:27,360
If you say, well, there's some probability distribution

554
00:36:27,360 --> 00:36:30,480
over a vast number of possible things,

555
00:36:30,480 --> 00:36:34,480
that doesn't give you very good guidance on what to do next.

556
00:36:34,480 --> 00:36:36,720
Whereas if you can say, oh, I think

557
00:36:36,720 --> 00:36:40,560
this patient is developing type 2 diabetes,

558
00:36:40,560 --> 00:36:44,680
then you're locked into a set of questions

559
00:36:44,680 --> 00:36:48,440
and a set of approaches that you might try.

560
00:36:48,440 --> 00:36:52,120
Now, when we come back to looking at machine learning,

561
00:36:52,120 --> 00:36:56,000
machines don't have the same limitations as people.

562
00:36:56,000 --> 00:36:59,520
And so for a machine to integrate over a vast number

563
00:36:59,520 --> 00:37:02,720
of possibilities is not difficult.

564
00:37:02,720 --> 00:37:06,800
But for a human cognition to do that is very hard.

565
00:37:06,800 --> 00:37:10,120
And so this is actually an important characteristic

566
00:37:10,160 --> 00:37:14,600
of the way doctors think about diagnostic reasoning.

567
00:37:14,600 --> 00:37:18,400
So then having made a diagnosis or made a guess,

568
00:37:18,400 --> 00:37:20,760
they plan some kind of therapy.

569
00:37:20,760 --> 00:37:23,160
They apply that therapy.

570
00:37:23,160 --> 00:37:26,560
And then they wait a while, and they see what happened.

571
00:37:26,560 --> 00:37:31,240
So if your diagnosis led you to a choice of therapy,

572
00:37:31,240 --> 00:37:33,360
you gave that therapy to the patient.

573
00:37:33,360 --> 00:37:35,360
And the patient got better.

574
00:37:35,360 --> 00:37:38,920
Then you say, well, it must have been the right diagnosis.

575
00:37:38,920 --> 00:37:41,080
If the patient didn't get better,

576
00:37:41,080 --> 00:37:44,680
then you say, well, how did what happened to the patient

577
00:37:44,680 --> 00:37:49,480
differ from what I expected to happen to the patient?

578
00:37:49,480 --> 00:37:54,000
And that drives your revision of this whole process.

579
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:57,080
So we, again, examine what happened

580
00:37:57,080 --> 00:37:58,960
as a result of the therapy.

581
00:37:58,960 --> 00:38:00,120
We gather more data.

582
00:38:00,120 --> 00:38:01,080
We interpret it.

583
00:38:01,080 --> 00:38:04,560
We come up with the revised diagnostic hypothesis.

584
00:38:04,560 --> 00:38:07,360
We come up with the revised therapeutic plan.

585
00:38:07,360 --> 00:38:11,080
And we keep going around the cycle.

586
00:38:11,080 --> 00:38:14,920
Now, that cycle happens very quickly

587
00:38:14,920 --> 00:38:17,320
if you're a hospitalized patient.

588
00:38:17,320 --> 00:38:19,160
Because you're there all the time.

589
00:38:19,160 --> 00:38:20,120
You're available.

590
00:38:20,120 --> 00:38:23,080
They're trying to do things to you constantly.

591
00:38:23,080 --> 00:38:28,560
And so this cycle happens on the order of hours or a day.

592
00:38:28,560 --> 00:38:31,120
Whereas if you're an outpatient and you're not

593
00:38:31,120 --> 00:38:32,960
dealing with some urgent problem,

594
00:38:32,960 --> 00:38:36,120
it may happen over a much slower period.

595
00:38:36,120 --> 00:38:39,120
It may be that your doctor says, well,

596
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:40,880
we're going to adjust your drug dose

597
00:38:40,880 --> 00:38:44,440
and see if that helps bring down your cholesterol

598
00:38:44,440 --> 00:38:46,800
or manage your pain or whatever it

599
00:38:46,800 --> 00:38:48,760
is that he's trying to do.

600
00:38:48,760 --> 00:38:51,240
Or worse yet, we're going to try to convince you

601
00:38:51,240 --> 00:38:53,320
to eat more healthy food.

602
00:38:53,320 --> 00:38:57,120
And six months later, we'll see if your hemoglobin A1c

603
00:38:57,120 --> 00:39:02,360
came down, that you're less close to being diabetic.

604
00:39:02,360 --> 00:39:04,600
So the timescale is very different.

605
00:39:04,600 --> 00:39:11,240
But that process of continually reinterpreting things

606
00:39:11,240 --> 00:39:13,200
is a really critical feature, I think,

607
00:39:13,200 --> 00:39:15,200
of all of medical care.

608
00:39:15,200 --> 00:39:20,680
And if you look back, Alan Turing actually

609
00:39:20,680 --> 00:39:25,280
talked in the early 1950s about health care

610
00:39:25,280 --> 00:39:27,560
as being one of the interesting application

611
00:39:27,560 --> 00:39:32,020
areas of artificial intelligence.

612
00:39:32,020 --> 00:39:32,600
Why?

613
00:39:32,600 --> 00:39:35,120
Well, because it was an important topic.

614
00:39:35,120 --> 00:39:37,120
And he had the vision that says,

615
00:39:37,120 --> 00:39:40,480
as we start getting more data about health care,

616
00:39:40,480 --> 00:39:43,000
we're going to be able to build the kinds of models

617
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:47,680
that we're going to be talking about in this class.

618
00:39:47,680 --> 00:39:53,880
But a lot of the early work took a kind of one-shot approach.

619
00:39:53,880 --> 00:39:57,440
So they said, well, we're going to solve the diagnostic problem.

620
00:39:57,440 --> 00:40:00,400
So we're going to take a snapshot of the patient,

621
00:40:00,400 --> 00:40:02,800
all their data at a particular moment,

622
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:04,520
we're going to feed it into an algorithm.

623
00:40:04,520 --> 00:40:06,680
It'll come up with the diagnosis.

624
00:40:06,680 --> 00:40:09,520
We're done.

625
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:13,080
And that wasn't very useful because it

626
00:40:13,080 --> 00:40:16,720
didn't obey the cyclic nature of the process

627
00:40:16,720 --> 00:40:18,760
of providing health care.

628
00:40:18,760 --> 00:40:22,480
And so this was a revolution that started really

629
00:40:22,480 --> 00:40:25,640
around the 1980s when people realized

630
00:40:25,640 --> 00:40:28,200
that you had to be in it for the long run

631
00:40:28,200 --> 00:40:31,560
and not for single interactions.

632
00:40:31,560 --> 00:40:37,680
OK, well, this is just some definitions

633
00:40:37,680 --> 00:40:40,200
of these care processes.

634
00:40:40,200 --> 00:40:46,640
So here I've listed some ideas that came from a 1976 paper

635
00:40:46,640 --> 00:40:50,800
by several of my colleagues who said, well,

636
00:40:50,800 --> 00:40:53,800
here's a cognitive theory of diagnosis

637
00:40:53,800 --> 00:40:59,280
from the initial complaints, guess a suitable hypothesis.

638
00:40:59,280 --> 00:41:03,720
Use the current active hypotheses to guide questioning.

639
00:41:03,720 --> 00:41:08,440
So to order more tests, to ask questions of the patient.

640
00:41:08,440 --> 00:41:11,480
And it's the failure to satisfy expectations

641
00:41:11,480 --> 00:41:14,840
that's the strongest clue to how to develop a better

642
00:41:14,840 --> 00:41:15,480
hypothesis.

643
00:41:18,560 --> 00:41:23,080
And then the hypotheses could be an activated, deactivated,

644
00:41:23,080 --> 00:41:25,800
confirmed, or rejected state.

645
00:41:25,800 --> 00:41:27,960
They actually built a computer program

646
00:41:27,960 --> 00:41:32,480
that implemented this theory of diagnostic reasoning.

647
00:41:32,480 --> 00:41:38,560
And these rules, essentially, about whether to activate,

648
00:41:38,560 --> 00:41:41,440
deactivate, confirm, or reject something

649
00:41:41,440 --> 00:41:44,360
could be based both on logical criteria

650
00:41:44,360 --> 00:41:49,160
and on a kind of very bad probabilistic model.

651
00:41:49,160 --> 00:41:52,680
So it was very bad because what they really needed

652
00:41:52,680 --> 00:41:54,520
was Bayesian networks.

653
00:41:54,520 --> 00:41:58,200
And those were about a decade in the future at that point.

654
00:41:58,200 --> 00:42:04,920
So they and every other system built in the 1970s

655
00:42:04,920 --> 00:42:07,360
had really horrible probabilistic models

656
00:42:07,360 --> 00:42:10,520
because we didn't understand how to do it correctly.

657
00:42:10,520 --> 00:42:12,040
Now, what's interesting is somebody

658
00:42:12,040 --> 00:42:16,400
noticed that if you strip away the medicine from this,

659
00:42:16,400 --> 00:42:20,800
this is kind of like the scientific method.

660
00:42:20,800 --> 00:42:23,360
If you're trying to understand something,

661
00:42:23,360 --> 00:42:26,680
you form a hypothesis, you perform an experiment.

662
00:42:26,680 --> 00:42:30,360
If the experiment is consistent with your expectations,

663
00:42:30,360 --> 00:42:33,440
then you go on and you've gotten a little bit more confident

664
00:42:33,440 --> 00:42:35,040
in your hypothesis.

665
00:42:35,040 --> 00:42:38,720
If your experiment is inconsistent with your expectations,

666
00:42:38,720 --> 00:42:42,520
then you have to change your theory, change your hypothesis.

667
00:42:42,520 --> 00:42:44,960
You go back and gather more data,

668
00:42:44,960 --> 00:42:47,400
and then keep doing this until you're satisfied

669
00:42:47,400 --> 00:42:50,200
that you've come up with an adequate theory.

670
00:42:50,200 --> 00:42:52,920
So this was a surprise to doctors

671
00:42:52,920 --> 00:42:56,440
because they thought of themselves more as artists

672
00:42:56,440 --> 00:42:58,040
than as scientists.

673
00:42:58,040 --> 00:43:00,520
But in a way, they act like scientists,

674
00:43:00,520 --> 00:43:03,040
which is kind of cool.

675
00:43:03,040 --> 00:43:08,320
All right, this doesn't stop with caring for a single patient.

676
00:43:08,320 --> 00:43:11,360
So we have all these meta-level processes

677
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:14,640
about the acquisition and the application of knowledge,

678
00:43:14,640 --> 00:43:18,600
about education, quality control, and process improvement,

679
00:43:18,600 --> 00:43:22,280
cost containment, and developing references.

680
00:43:22,280 --> 00:43:25,480
So this is a picture from David Margulies, who

681
00:43:25,480 --> 00:43:29,840
was chief information officer at Children's Hospital here.

682
00:43:29,840 --> 00:43:34,520
And the cycle that I described is the one right here.

683
00:43:34,520 --> 00:43:38,200
This is the care team taking care of a patient.

684
00:43:38,200 --> 00:43:42,440
But of course, at some point, that patient is discharged.

685
00:43:42,440 --> 00:43:46,120
And then they're in community care and self-care.

686
00:43:46,120 --> 00:43:48,760
And then maybe they're in some sort of active health

687
00:43:48,760 --> 00:43:50,480
status management.

688
00:43:50,480 --> 00:43:54,440
And then if that goes badly, then there's

689
00:43:54,440 --> 00:43:58,120
some episode where they reconnect to the health care

690
00:43:58,120 --> 00:43:59,120
system.

691
00:43:59,120 --> 00:44:01,920
They get authorized to come back.

692
00:44:01,920 --> 00:44:04,200
They get scheduled for a visit.

693
00:44:04,200 --> 00:44:06,240
And they're back in the cycle.

694
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:11,840
And so the process of care involves people going

695
00:44:11,840 --> 00:44:14,560
to the hospital, getting taken care of.

696
00:44:14,560 --> 00:44:15,760
They get better.

697
00:44:15,760 --> 00:44:17,040
They get discharged.

698
00:44:17,040 --> 00:44:19,360
They live in their lives for a while.

699
00:44:19,360 --> 00:44:21,080
Maybe they get sick again.

700
00:44:21,080 --> 00:44:21,960
They come back.

701
00:44:21,960 --> 00:44:26,880
And so there's this larger cycle around that issue.

702
00:44:26,880 --> 00:44:29,920
And then around that are all kinds

703
00:44:29,920 --> 00:44:33,760
of things about health plan design and membership

704
00:44:33,760 --> 00:44:38,400
and what coverage you have and so on.

705
00:44:38,400 --> 00:44:41,600
And then I would add one more idea,

706
00:44:41,600 --> 00:44:45,680
which is that if you have a system like this,

707
00:44:45,720 --> 00:44:50,360
you actually want to study at the next meta level

708
00:44:50,360 --> 00:44:55,760
that system, make observations about it, analyze it, model it,

709
00:44:55,760 --> 00:44:59,880
plan some improvements, and then intervene in the system,

710
00:44:59,880 --> 00:45:05,360
and observe how it's working and try to make it better.

711
00:45:05,360 --> 00:45:11,360
So in terms of tasks that are important for us in this class,

712
00:45:11,360 --> 00:45:16,520
this class of tasks is central.

713
00:45:16,520 --> 00:45:19,880
Because one of the things we're trying to do

714
00:45:19,880 --> 00:45:21,960
is to look at the way health care works

715
00:45:21,960 --> 00:45:24,240
and to figure out how to make it better

716
00:45:24,240 --> 00:45:26,480
by examining its operation.

717
00:45:26,480 --> 00:45:29,320
And that can be done at any of these three levels.

718
00:45:29,320 --> 00:45:32,680
It can be done in the more acute phase

719
00:45:32,680 --> 00:45:34,440
where we're dealing with somebody who's

720
00:45:34,440 --> 00:45:36,080
in the middle of an illness.

721
00:45:36,080 --> 00:45:39,600
It can be done at the larger phase of somebody

722
00:45:39,600 --> 00:45:42,640
who's going through the cycle of being well for a while

723
00:45:42,640 --> 00:45:45,360
and then being sick and then being well again

724
00:45:45,360 --> 00:45:46,920
and being sick again.

725
00:45:46,920 --> 00:45:51,040
And it can be in terms of the system itself

726
00:45:51,040 --> 00:45:53,920
of how do you design a health care system that

727
00:45:53,920 --> 00:45:56,200
works better for the population.

728
00:45:56,200 --> 00:45:59,080
So this notion of a learning health care system

729
00:45:59,080 --> 00:46:01,280
is now a journal.

730
00:46:01,280 --> 00:46:05,800
So in 2017, Chuck Friedman at the University of Michigan

731
00:46:05,800 --> 00:46:08,120
started this new journal.

732
00:46:08,120 --> 00:46:13,360
And it's full of articles about this third level

733
00:46:13,360 --> 00:46:17,680
of external cycle.

734
00:46:17,680 --> 00:46:20,960
So how does the health care system learn?

735
00:46:20,960 --> 00:46:23,880
Well, I'll tell you an anecdote.

736
00:46:23,880 --> 00:46:30,800
In the mid 1980s, I was teaching an AI expert systems course.

737
00:46:30,800 --> 00:46:33,400
And I had just come back from a conference

738
00:46:33,400 --> 00:46:36,000
of medical informatics people where

739
00:46:36,000 --> 00:46:39,200
they were talking about this great new idea called

740
00:46:39,200 --> 00:46:42,080
evidence-based medicine.

741
00:46:42,080 --> 00:46:45,640
And I remember mentioning this to a bunch of MIT engineering

742
00:46:45,640 --> 00:46:46,240
students.

743
00:46:46,240 --> 00:46:51,280
And one of them raised his hand and said, as opposed to what?

744
00:46:51,280 --> 00:46:52,280
Right?

745
00:46:52,280 --> 00:46:54,520
I mean, to an engineer, it's obvious

746
00:46:54,520 --> 00:46:58,640
that evidence is the basis on which you analyze things

747
00:46:58,640 --> 00:47:00,360
and make things better.

748
00:47:00,360 --> 00:47:03,080
But it wasn't obvious to doctors.

749
00:47:03,080 --> 00:47:06,680
And so this was almost a revolutionary change.

750
00:47:06,680 --> 00:47:09,120
And the idea that they fostered was

751
00:47:09,120 --> 00:47:13,360
the idea of the randomized controlled clinical trial.

752
00:47:13,360 --> 00:47:17,040
So I'm going to sketch what that looks like.

753
00:47:17,040 --> 00:47:18,720
Of course, there are many variations.

754
00:47:18,720 --> 00:47:21,840
But suppose that I'm one of the drug companies

755
00:47:21,840 --> 00:47:23,880
around Kendall Square.

756
00:47:23,880 --> 00:47:26,040
And I come up with a new drug, and I

757
00:47:26,040 --> 00:47:28,760
want to prove that it's more effective for condition

758
00:47:28,760 --> 00:47:32,520
X than some existing drug B.

759
00:47:32,520 --> 00:47:34,160
So what do I do?

760
00:47:34,160 --> 00:47:38,080
I find some patients who are suffering from X.

761
00:47:38,080 --> 00:47:41,040
And I try very hard to find patients who are not

762
00:47:41,040 --> 00:47:43,280
suffering from anything else.

763
00:47:43,280 --> 00:47:46,320
I want the purest case possible.

764
00:47:46,320 --> 00:47:48,640
I then go to my statisticians.

765
00:47:48,640 --> 00:47:52,160
And I say, let's design an experiment

766
00:47:52,160 --> 00:47:55,600
where we're going to collect a standard set of data

767
00:47:55,600 --> 00:47:57,920
about all these patients.

768
00:47:57,920 --> 00:48:00,080
And then we're going to give some of them

769
00:48:01,040 --> 00:48:04,080
drug A and some of them drug B. And we'll

770
00:48:04,080 --> 00:48:06,240
see which of them do better.

771
00:48:06,240 --> 00:48:10,160
And we'll pre-define what we mean by do better.

772
00:48:10,160 --> 00:48:13,160
So like not dying is considered doing better

773
00:48:13,160 --> 00:48:17,960
or not suffering some bad thing that people are suffering from

774
00:48:17,960 --> 00:48:21,680
is considered doing better.

775
00:48:21,680 --> 00:48:25,240
And then the statisticians also will

776
00:48:25,240 --> 00:48:29,880
tell me, given that you expect drug A

777
00:48:29,880 --> 00:48:33,800
to be, let's say, 10% better than drug B,

778
00:48:33,800 --> 00:48:37,480
how many patients do you have to enroll in this trial

779
00:48:37,480 --> 00:48:40,960
in order to be likely to get a statistically significant

780
00:48:40,960 --> 00:48:43,680
answer to that question?

781
00:48:43,680 --> 00:48:45,360
And then they do it.

782
00:48:45,360 --> 00:48:48,240
The statisticians analyze the data.

783
00:48:48,240 --> 00:48:51,600
Hopefully, you've gotten P less than 0.05.

784
00:48:51,600 --> 00:48:53,680
You go to the Food and Drug Administration

785
00:48:53,720 --> 00:48:56,880
and say, give me permission to market this drug

786
00:48:56,880 --> 00:49:00,640
as the hottest new cure for something or other.

787
00:49:00,640 --> 00:49:03,520
And then you make billions of dollars.

788
00:49:03,520 --> 00:49:07,480
This is the standard way that pharma works.

789
00:49:07,480 --> 00:49:09,760
Now, there are some problems.

790
00:49:09,760 --> 00:49:17,280
So most of the cases to which the results of a trial like this

791
00:49:17,280 --> 00:49:23,200
are applied wouldn't have qualified to be in the trial.

792
00:49:23,200 --> 00:49:28,000
Because, for example, we talked about morbidities,

793
00:49:28,000 --> 00:49:31,880
about chronic problems that people have.

794
00:49:31,880 --> 00:49:34,160
Well, if you're dealing with one disease,

795
00:49:34,160 --> 00:49:36,640
you want to make sure that those populations you're

796
00:49:36,640 --> 00:49:39,800
dealing with don't have any of these other diseases.

797
00:49:39,800 --> 00:49:42,360
But in the real world, people do.

798
00:49:42,360 --> 00:49:44,480
And so we've never actually measured

799
00:49:44,480 --> 00:49:47,840
what happens to those people if you give them the drug

800
00:49:47,840 --> 00:49:52,040
and they have these comorbidities.

801
00:49:52,040 --> 00:49:54,440
The other problem is that the drug company

802
00:49:54,440 --> 00:49:57,440
wants to start making these billions of dollars

803
00:49:57,440 --> 00:49:59,520
as soon as possible.

804
00:49:59,520 --> 00:50:03,760
And so they want the trial to be as short as possible.

805
00:50:03,760 --> 00:50:06,480
And they want it to be as small a sample

806
00:50:06,480 --> 00:50:11,240
as they need to get that 0.05 statistical significance.

807
00:50:11,240 --> 00:50:13,960
So these are all problematic.

808
00:50:13,960 --> 00:50:17,320
And they lead to real problems.

809
00:50:17,320 --> 00:50:19,200
So I didn't bring any examples.

810
00:50:19,200 --> 00:50:23,400
But there are plenty of stories where the FDA has approved

811
00:50:23,400 --> 00:50:25,400
some drug on this basis.

812
00:50:25,400 --> 00:50:30,120
And then later they discovered that although the drug works

813
00:50:30,120 --> 00:50:32,920
well in the short term, it has horrible side

814
00:50:32,920 --> 00:50:34,720
effects in the long term.

815
00:50:34,720 --> 00:50:38,320
Or that it has interactions with other diseases

816
00:50:38,320 --> 00:50:40,960
where it doesn't work effectively for people,

817
00:50:40,960 --> 00:50:45,600
except for these pure cases that it was tested on.

818
00:50:45,640 --> 00:50:48,960
So the other idea, the competing idea,

819
00:50:48,960 --> 00:50:53,160
is to say, let's build this learning health care system in

820
00:50:53,160 --> 00:50:57,120
which progress in science informatics, whatever,

821
00:50:57,120 --> 00:51:00,920
generates new knowledge as an ongoing natural byproduct

822
00:51:00,920 --> 00:51:02,880
of the care experience.

823
00:51:02,880 --> 00:51:07,240
And we seamlessly refine and deliver best practices

824
00:51:07,240 --> 00:51:10,400
for continuous improvement in health and health care.

825
00:51:10,400 --> 00:51:13,200
Wonderful words from the Institute of Medicine,

826
00:51:13,200 --> 00:51:17,760
now called the National Academy of Medicine.

827
00:51:17,760 --> 00:51:19,800
But it's hard to do this.

828
00:51:19,800 --> 00:51:21,680
And the reason it's hard to do this

829
00:51:21,680 --> 00:51:27,240
is mainly for a very profound underlying reason, which

830
00:51:27,240 --> 00:51:32,280
is that people are not treated by experimental protocols.

831
00:51:32,280 --> 00:51:35,560
So it's very important in that sketch

832
00:51:35,560 --> 00:51:39,160
that I gave you of the drug A versus drug B

833
00:51:39,160 --> 00:51:44,000
that there is a randomization step where I flip a coin

834
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:47,200
to decide which drug any particular individual is

835
00:51:47,200 --> 00:51:49,240
going to get.

836
00:51:49,240 --> 00:51:55,160
If I allow that decision to be biased by my expectations

837
00:51:55,160 --> 00:51:58,040
or by something else I know about the patient,

838
00:51:58,040 --> 00:52:01,320
then I'm no longer doing a fair trial.

839
00:52:01,320 --> 00:52:03,640
And of course, when I collect data

840
00:52:03,640 --> 00:52:06,880
about how actual patients are being treated,

841
00:52:06,880 --> 00:52:10,040
they're being treated according to what their doctors think

842
00:52:10,040 --> 00:52:11,720
is best for them.

843
00:52:11,720 --> 00:52:14,440
And so there is no randomization.

844
00:52:14,440 --> 00:52:16,960
I mean, if I went to Mass General and said,

845
00:52:16,960 --> 00:52:19,520
could you guys please treat everybody randomly

846
00:52:19,520 --> 00:52:23,320
so that we collect really good data,

847
00:52:23,320 --> 00:52:27,400
they would throw me up properly.

848
00:52:27,400 --> 00:52:32,560
OK, so we also need a whole lot of technical infrastructure.

849
00:52:32,560 --> 00:52:35,800
We need to capture all kinds of novel data sources, which

850
00:52:35,840 --> 00:52:38,800
we'll talk about in the next lecture.

851
00:52:38,800 --> 00:52:41,000
And then we need a technical infrastructure

852
00:52:41,000 --> 00:52:42,800
for truly big data.

853
00:52:42,800 --> 00:52:48,360
So just, for example, the Dana-Farber

854
00:52:48,360 --> 00:52:51,280
started about five years ago.

855
00:52:51,280 --> 00:52:52,440
It's a cancer hospital.

856
00:52:52,440 --> 00:52:55,000
And so for every solid tumor, they

857
00:52:55,000 --> 00:52:58,720
would take samples of the tumor and genotype it,

858
00:52:58,720 --> 00:53:02,880
multiple samples because tumors are not uniform.

859
00:53:02,880 --> 00:53:06,280
So just storing that stuff is a technical challenge

860
00:53:06,280 --> 00:53:09,000
and being able to come up with it.

861
00:53:09,000 --> 00:53:16,280
You've got three gigabases, so about over a gigabyte of data,

862
00:53:16,280 --> 00:53:19,120
from each sample, from each tumor,

863
00:53:19,120 --> 00:53:23,480
times all the people who come in and have this test.

864
00:53:23,480 --> 00:53:26,440
So you buy some big disk drives or you

865
00:53:26,440 --> 00:53:28,560
farm it out to Google or something.

866
00:53:28,560 --> 00:53:30,880
But then you need to organize it in some way

867
00:53:30,880 --> 00:53:36,240
so that it's usefully easy to find that data.

868
00:53:36,240 --> 00:53:40,560
So today's technique, today's prejudice,

869
00:53:40,560 --> 00:53:44,920
is what I call the meat grinder story, which

870
00:53:44,920 --> 00:53:47,120
is you take medical records, genetic data,

871
00:53:47,120 --> 00:53:50,240
environmental data, data from wearables.

872
00:53:50,240 --> 00:53:53,240
You put them into an old fashioned meat grinder

873
00:53:53,240 --> 00:53:56,800
and outcome bits, which you store on a disk.

874
00:53:56,800 --> 00:54:00,480
And then you have all the data from which you can build models.

875
00:54:00,680 --> 00:54:01,600
And that's what we do.

876
00:54:01,600 --> 00:54:06,000
And you're going to see a lot of that in this course.

877
00:54:06,000 --> 00:54:08,040
OK.

878
00:54:08,040 --> 00:54:11,100
The other thing that medicine tries to do

879
00:54:11,100 --> 00:54:14,440
is not to cure people, but to keep them healthy.

880
00:54:14,440 --> 00:54:19,240
And this has been pretty much the domain of the public health

881
00:54:19,240 --> 00:54:20,760
infrastructure.

882
00:54:20,760 --> 00:54:25,080
So if you go across the river to the Harvard Medical Area,

883
00:54:25,080 --> 00:54:26,760
there are a couple of big buildings, which

884
00:54:26,760 --> 00:54:28,560
is the Harvard School of Public Health.

885
00:54:28,560 --> 00:54:31,640
And this is what they're all about.

886
00:54:31,640 --> 00:54:35,520
So they do things like tracking disease prevalence

887
00:54:35,520 --> 00:54:39,320
and tracking infections and worrying about quarantining

888
00:54:39,320 --> 00:54:40,160
people.

889
00:54:40,160 --> 00:54:42,360
They also do a lot of this kind of work

890
00:54:42,360 --> 00:54:44,860
we're going to talk about in this class, which

891
00:54:44,860 --> 00:54:47,920
is modeling in order to try to understand what's

892
00:54:47,920 --> 00:54:52,640
going on in individuals' health, in the health of a population,

893
00:54:52,640 --> 00:54:55,640
in the operations of a health care system.

894
00:54:55,640 --> 00:54:57,840
So they're very much into this.

895
00:54:57,840 --> 00:55:03,360
Now historically, I look back and it turns out

896
00:55:03,360 --> 00:55:07,200
there's something called the London Bills of Mortality

897
00:55:07,200 --> 00:55:12,920
in the 17th century, started by a gentleman named John Grant.

898
00:55:12,920 --> 00:55:17,240
And he was interested just in figuring out

899
00:55:17,240 --> 00:55:20,120
how long do people live.

900
00:55:20,120 --> 00:55:24,040
And so he came up with these Bills of Mortality

901
00:55:24,040 --> 00:55:26,960
where he went around to different parts of London,

902
00:55:26,960 --> 00:55:29,800
talked to undertakers and hospitals

903
00:55:29,800 --> 00:55:34,160
and whatever health care providers existed at the time,

904
00:55:34,160 --> 00:55:38,240
and collected data on what people died

905
00:55:38,240 --> 00:55:40,800
and how many people were living in that area.

906
00:55:40,800 --> 00:55:46,560
And so for example, he estimated that the mortality before age

907
00:55:46,560 --> 00:55:54,760
six in the 17th century, a long time ago, 1600s, was about 36%.

908
00:55:54,760 --> 00:56:00,080
So if you were a kid, your chances of making it to age six

909
00:56:00,080 --> 00:56:04,120
were only about 64%.

910
00:56:04,120 --> 00:56:06,400
It's a less than 2 thirds.

911
00:56:06,400 --> 00:56:08,880
It's kind of shocking.

912
00:56:08,880 --> 00:56:13,720
In the 18th century, people you've never heard of and Linnaeus,

913
00:56:13,720 --> 00:56:15,480
who you probably have heard of because he

914
00:56:15,480 --> 00:56:20,840
was one of the early taxonomists for biological and animal

915
00:56:20,840 --> 00:56:23,880
species and so on, made the first attempt

916
00:56:23,880 --> 00:56:26,600
at systemic classification.

917
00:56:26,600 --> 00:56:31,160
In the mid-1850s, mid-1800s, there

918
00:56:31,160 --> 00:56:34,960
was a Congress of the First International Statistical

919
00:56:34,960 --> 00:56:38,960
Congress, and a gentleman named William Farr

920
00:56:38,960 --> 00:56:43,320
came up with an interesting categorization that said,

921
00:56:43,320 --> 00:56:47,040
well, if we're going to taxonomize diseases,

922
00:56:47,040 --> 00:56:50,040
we should separate epidemic diseases

923
00:56:50,040 --> 00:56:54,480
from constitutional diseases, from local diseases,

924
00:56:54,480 --> 00:56:57,960
whereby local he means affecting a particular part

925
00:56:57,960 --> 00:57:02,720
of the body, from developmental diseases.

926
00:57:02,720 --> 00:57:06,080
So this is things like being stunted growth

927
00:57:06,080 --> 00:57:10,920
or failure of mental development or speech development,

928
00:57:10,920 --> 00:57:14,000
and then diseases that are the direct result of violence.

929
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:18,880
So this is things like broken bones and bar fight results

930
00:57:18,880 --> 00:57:21,000
and stuff like that.

931
00:57:21,000 --> 00:57:24,400
So that was the first classification

932
00:57:24,400 --> 00:57:28,800
of disease in about 1853.

933
00:57:28,800 --> 00:57:32,160
Note, by the way, that this is before Louis Pasteur

934
00:57:32,160 --> 00:57:36,160
and his theory of the germ cause of disease.

935
00:57:36,160 --> 00:57:38,520
And so this was a pretty early attempt

936
00:57:38,520 --> 00:57:40,880
and obviously could have benefited

937
00:57:40,880 --> 00:57:45,000
from what Pasteur later discovered.

938
00:57:45,040 --> 00:57:49,240
So by the 1890s, which is post Pasteur,

939
00:57:49,240 --> 00:57:51,720
they came up with a classification

940
00:57:51,720 --> 00:57:54,040
that was a hierarchic classification,

941
00:57:54,040 --> 00:57:58,280
44 top-level hierarchies, broken up

942
00:57:58,280 --> 00:58:04,400
into 99 lower-level categories and 161 particular titles.

943
00:58:04,400 --> 00:58:09,280
And they adopted this as a way of getting typically mortality

944
00:58:09,280 --> 00:58:12,840
data of what was it that people were dying of.

945
00:58:12,840 --> 00:58:20,000
And by 1920s, you've heard of ICD, ICD-9, ICD-10.

946
00:58:20,000 --> 00:58:23,960
So this is currently used as a way of classifying diseases

947
00:58:23,960 --> 00:58:26,560
and disorders.

948
00:58:26,560 --> 00:58:30,400
The international list of the causes of death

949
00:58:30,400 --> 00:58:35,400
was the first ICD back in, I think, the 1920s.

950
00:58:35,400 --> 00:58:39,960
And then it kept developing through multiple versions.

951
00:58:39,960 --> 00:58:46,680
In 1975, ICD-9 was adopted in 2015, ICD-10.

952
00:58:46,680 --> 00:58:50,360
And these are under the control of the World Health Organization,

953
00:58:50,360 --> 00:58:52,760
which is now a UN agency, although I think

954
00:58:52,760 --> 00:58:56,320
it predates the UN, actually.

955
00:58:56,320 --> 00:59:01,800
And then we have ICD-9-CM and ICD-10-CM are US clinical

956
00:59:01,800 --> 00:59:07,000
modifications that are an extension of the ICD-9 and 10

957
00:59:07,000 --> 00:59:08,320
coding.

958
00:59:08,320 --> 00:59:12,160
And they are primarily used for billing,

959
00:59:12,160 --> 00:59:15,520
but they're also used for epidemiological research.

960
00:59:15,520 --> 00:59:21,040
And if you look at the Centers for Disease Control, CDC,

961
00:59:21,040 --> 00:59:24,320
they collect death certificates like this

962
00:59:24,320 --> 00:59:26,080
from all over the country.

963
00:59:26,080 --> 00:59:31,000
And so this is a person who died of a cerebral hemorrhage, which

964
00:59:31,000 --> 00:59:35,160
was due to nephritis, which was due to cirrhosis of the liver.

965
00:59:35,160 --> 00:59:37,760
And so you can use this kind of data

966
00:59:37,760 --> 00:59:40,840
to say, well, here's the immediate cause of death,

967
00:59:40,840 --> 00:59:43,240
here's sort of the proximate cause of death,

968
00:59:43,240 --> 00:59:47,040
and here's the underlying cause of death.

969
00:59:47,040 --> 00:59:50,320
And so this is the sort of statistical data

970
00:59:50,320 --> 00:59:52,840
that we now have available.

971
00:59:52,840 --> 00:59:56,760
Now, do any of you watch the PBS show, Victoria?

972
00:59:59,640 --> 01:00:02,720
No, but you're not television watchers.

973
01:00:02,720 --> 01:00:04,360
All right, it's pretty cool.

974
01:00:04,360 --> 01:00:12,400
So I was stunned, because as I was preparing this lecture

975
01:00:12,400 --> 01:00:15,560
and I had the next slide, it turns out

976
01:00:15,560 --> 01:00:18,960
this plays a role in one of the episodes that was broadcast

977
01:00:18,960 --> 01:00:21,200
about a week and a half ago.

978
01:00:21,200 --> 01:00:30,080
So in the 1850s, there was a big outbreak of cholera in London.

979
01:00:30,080 --> 01:00:36,880
And John Snow was a doctor who did this amazing epidemiological

980
01:00:36,880 --> 01:00:40,840
study to try to figure out what was causing cholera.

981
01:00:40,840 --> 01:00:47,440
The accepted opinion was the cholera was caused by miasma.

982
01:00:47,440 --> 01:00:48,400
What's a miasma?

983
01:00:51,160 --> 01:00:53,360
Bad air, OK?

984
01:00:53,360 --> 01:00:54,640
So it's bad air.

985
01:00:54,640 --> 01:01:00,720
Somehow, bad air was causing people to get sick and die.

986
01:01:00,720 --> 01:01:05,480
And several hundred people died, whereas Snow

987
01:01:05,480 --> 01:01:09,160
started plotting on a map of the area in London

988
01:01:09,160 --> 01:01:13,640
where these were concentrated, where everybody lived.

989
01:01:13,640 --> 01:01:16,680
And what he discovered, interestingly enough,

990
01:01:16,680 --> 01:01:20,360
is that right in the middle of Broad Street, which is pretty

991
01:01:20,360 --> 01:01:24,600
much at the epicenter of all these people dying,

992
01:01:24,600 --> 01:01:29,000
was a water pump that everybody in the neighborhood used.

993
01:01:29,000 --> 01:01:35,040
And that water pump, its supply had become infected by cholera.

994
01:01:35,040 --> 01:01:38,800
And so people were pumping water, taking it home, drinking it,

995
01:01:38,800 --> 01:01:42,600
and then dying, or at least getting very sick.

996
01:01:42,600 --> 01:01:48,480
And he looked at this and he said, well, if we turn off

997
01:01:48,480 --> 01:01:52,600
that pump, the epidemic will stop.

998
01:01:52,600 --> 01:01:55,880
And he actually went to the Queen, Queen Victoria,

999
01:01:55,880 --> 01:01:59,200
hence the tie into the television show,

1000
01:01:59,200 --> 01:02:03,000
and convinced her that this was worth trying

1001
01:02:03,000 --> 01:02:05,640
because they didn't have any better ideas.

1002
01:02:05,640 --> 01:02:08,000
And they took the pump handle off the pump

1003
01:02:08,000 --> 01:02:12,040
and sure enough, the cholera epidemic abated.

1004
01:02:12,040 --> 01:02:17,280
Now, of course, the underlying problem was sanitation.

1005
01:02:17,280 --> 01:02:18,600
And they didn't fix that.

1006
01:02:18,600 --> 01:02:21,520
That took longer.

1007
01:02:21,520 --> 01:02:27,200
But what's interesting is here is a 2003 study

1008
01:02:27,200 --> 01:02:30,480
of the spread of West Nile virus.

1009
01:02:30,480 --> 01:02:34,800
So this is mosquitoes that are biting people

1010
01:02:34,800 --> 01:02:38,640
and infecting them with this nasty disease.

1011
01:02:38,640 --> 01:02:41,960
And they actually used very similar techniques

1012
01:02:41,960 --> 01:02:46,360
to figure out that maybe this was coming in on airplanes

1013
01:02:46,400 --> 01:02:48,320
through JFK.

1014
01:02:48,320 --> 01:02:53,280
So mosquitoes were hitching a ride on an airplane

1015
01:02:53,280 --> 01:02:55,800
and coming into the US.

1016
01:02:55,800 --> 01:03:01,240
We need to build a border wall against mosquitoes.

1017
01:03:04,760 --> 01:03:08,160
There's also a very controversial practice

1018
01:03:08,160 --> 01:03:11,880
that used to be used a lot by public health officials, which

1019
01:03:11,880 --> 01:03:14,280
was to quarantine people.

1020
01:03:14,280 --> 01:03:18,080
And so there are lots of examples.

1021
01:03:18,080 --> 01:03:22,200
Anybody's relatives come through Ellis Island?

1022
01:03:22,200 --> 01:03:23,640
Must be a few.

1023
01:03:23,640 --> 01:03:27,240
OK, so they were subject to quarantine.

1024
01:03:27,240 --> 01:03:30,480
If you were sick when you arrived at Ellis Island

1025
01:03:30,480 --> 01:03:33,640
and they didn't know exactly how sick you were,

1026
01:03:33,640 --> 01:03:36,840
they would put you in a building and wait a month

1027
01:03:36,840 --> 01:03:39,520
and see if you got better or if you got worse

1028
01:03:39,520 --> 01:03:42,880
and then decide whether to send you back or to let you in.

1029
01:03:42,880 --> 01:03:45,080
So that was a pretty common practice.

1030
01:03:45,080 --> 01:03:47,960
There's a famous story of Typhoid Mary,

1031
01:03:47,960 --> 01:03:50,640
who was a carrier of typhoid fever,

1032
01:03:50,640 --> 01:03:53,280
but was not herself affected.

1033
01:03:53,280 --> 01:03:55,480
Unfortunately, she was a cook.

1034
01:03:55,480 --> 01:04:00,200
And wherever she was employed, people got really sick.

1035
01:04:00,200 --> 01:04:03,680
And eventually, the New York Department of Health

1036
01:04:03,680 --> 01:04:09,800
forcibly essentially jailed her in some sanatorium

1037
01:04:09,800 --> 01:04:13,240
in order to keep her from continuing to infect people.

1038
01:04:13,240 --> 01:04:17,160
It's a very controversial case, as you might imagine.

1039
01:04:17,160 --> 01:04:18,880
You don't have to go that far back.

1040
01:04:18,880 --> 01:04:25,200
Here's a 1987 article from UPI from the Chicago Tribune.

1041
01:04:25,200 --> 01:04:28,200
So Jesse Helms, who was a senator,

1042
01:04:28,200 --> 01:04:33,520
was calling for everybody who has AIDS to be quarantined.

1043
01:04:33,520 --> 01:04:36,240
So fortunately, that didn't happen.

1044
01:04:36,240 --> 01:04:40,840
But the idea is still around to say, well,

1045
01:04:40,840 --> 01:04:45,320
we're going to stop this infection by quarantining people.

1046
01:04:45,320 --> 01:04:51,800
And then here's a recent report about the Ebola response

1047
01:04:51,800 --> 01:04:55,320
in Africa over the past few years when

1048
01:04:55,320 --> 01:04:59,240
Ebola was ravaging parts of that continent.

1049
01:04:59,240 --> 01:05:03,800
And their conclusions are it's controversial and debated

1050
01:05:03,800 --> 01:05:07,200
issues, significant risks related to human rights.

1051
01:05:07,200 --> 01:05:10,000
Quarantine should be used as a last resort.

1052
01:05:10,000 --> 01:05:13,560
Quarantines in urban areas are really hard.

1053
01:05:13,560 --> 01:05:15,880
Mobile populations make it hard.

1054
01:05:15,880 --> 01:05:20,920
And this is the most technical conclusion

1055
01:05:20,920 --> 01:05:23,320
that if you're going to quarantine a bunch of people,

1056
01:05:23,320 --> 01:05:26,640
you have a huge waste disposal problem on your hands.

1057
01:05:26,640 --> 01:05:29,720
Because if you have people who might have Ebola,

1058
01:05:29,720 --> 01:05:31,880
you can't just take their garbage

1059
01:05:31,880 --> 01:05:33,720
and throw it out somewhere.

1060
01:05:33,720 --> 01:05:36,680
You have to dispose of it properly.

1061
01:05:36,680 --> 01:05:39,920
OK, so the last thing I wanted to talk about,

1062
01:05:39,920 --> 01:05:45,160
hopefully mercifully short, will be paying for health care.

1063
01:05:45,160 --> 01:05:49,800
So I remember reading about 20 years ago

1064
01:05:49,800 --> 01:05:53,280
that if you bought a Chevy from General Motors,

1065
01:05:53,280 --> 01:05:57,000
they spent more money on health insurance and health care

1066
01:05:57,000 --> 01:06:00,560
for their employees than they did on the steel that

1067
01:06:00,560 --> 01:06:02,680
went into your car.

1068
01:06:02,680 --> 01:06:05,080
That's pretty amazing.

1069
01:06:05,080 --> 01:06:07,480
And so why is this?

1070
01:06:07,480 --> 01:06:10,440
Well, essentially, there's an insatiable demand

1071
01:06:10,440 --> 01:06:12,800
for health care, right?

1072
01:06:12,800 --> 01:06:17,960
Nobody wants to die except for the suicidal people.

1073
01:06:17,960 --> 01:06:22,880
And so if I'm sick, I want the best care possible.

1074
01:06:22,880 --> 01:06:26,320
And I want as much of it as possible.

1075
01:06:26,320 --> 01:06:29,960
Because what is more important in life

1076
01:06:29,960 --> 01:06:32,960
than continuing to live?

1077
01:06:32,960 --> 01:06:38,520
So we also have gotten better at making drugs and better

1078
01:06:38,520 --> 01:06:40,480
tests and so on.

1079
01:06:40,480 --> 01:06:47,080
I remember when MRI machines became popular about 30 years ago.

1080
01:06:47,080 --> 01:06:49,080
The state of Massachusetts, for example,

1081
01:06:49,080 --> 01:06:53,240
had a commission that you had to convince them

1082
01:06:53,240 --> 01:06:57,400
to be allowed to buy an MRI machine for your hospital.

1083
01:06:57,400 --> 01:07:00,600
Because MRI machines are very expensive.

1084
01:07:00,600 --> 01:07:04,280
And MRIs were, at that time, hugely expensive.

1085
01:07:04,280 --> 01:07:07,440
And so they wanted to contain the cost of health care

1086
01:07:07,440 --> 01:07:10,040
by limiting the number of such machines.

1087
01:07:10,040 --> 01:07:12,000
Well, eventually, the cost came down.

1088
01:07:12,000 --> 01:07:13,560
And so we're doing better.

1089
01:07:13,560 --> 01:07:15,120
But if you read the newspapers, you

1090
01:07:15,120 --> 01:07:18,480
see that drug therapy is very expensive.

1091
01:07:18,480 --> 01:07:24,080
We have these wonder drugs for rare diseases or cancers

1092
01:07:24,080 --> 01:07:29,520
that cost $1 million a year to pay for your dosage.

1093
01:07:29,520 --> 01:07:34,400
And so there is a high human motivation to do this

1094
01:07:34,400 --> 01:07:39,000
and not much pushback except from insurance companies.

1095
01:07:39,000 --> 01:07:43,440
But they just passed the cost on in insurance contracts.

1096
01:07:43,440 --> 01:07:45,640
There's also waste.

1097
01:07:45,640 --> 01:07:49,360
So there are lots of stories about half of health care

1098
01:07:49,360 --> 01:07:54,120
expenses are spent in the last year of somebody's life.

1099
01:07:54,120 --> 01:07:57,400
Although Inglefinger, who was the editor of the New England

1100
01:07:57,400 --> 01:08:00,760
Journal, gave a talk here about 25 years ago.

1101
01:08:00,760 --> 01:08:03,480
And he said, you know, when I was a practicing doctor,

1102
01:08:03,480 --> 01:08:06,240
no patient ever came into my office saying, Doc,

1103
01:08:06,240 --> 01:08:09,240
I'm in the last year of my life.

1104
01:08:09,240 --> 01:08:12,920
And so that was a difficult criterion

1105
01:08:12,920 --> 01:08:15,680
to try to operationalize.

1106
01:08:15,680 --> 01:08:18,400
There are marginally useful procedures.

1107
01:08:18,400 --> 01:08:22,920
The IOM estimated that there are sort of 40 to 100,000 quote

1108
01:08:22,920 --> 01:08:26,560
unquote, unnecessary deaths per year.

1109
01:08:26,560 --> 01:08:29,160
In other words, deaths that could be avoided

1110
01:08:29,160 --> 01:08:32,520
by just being more careful and a little bit smarter.

1111
01:08:32,520 --> 01:08:35,880
Well, so the result of this is that if you

1112
01:08:35,880 --> 01:08:40,280
look at health care spending as a percentage of gross domestic

1113
01:08:40,280 --> 01:08:45,440
product from 1970 to, I think, 2017, I believe,

1114
01:08:45,440 --> 01:08:48,320
on this graph, what you see is,

1115
01:08:48,760 --> 01:08:54,440
one real outlier, and that's the United States on top.

1116
01:08:54,440 --> 01:08:59,160
So I've selected a few of these just to look at.

1117
01:08:59,160 --> 01:09:01,000
There's the US on top.

1118
01:09:01,000 --> 01:09:05,120
France, Germany, a lot of the European countries

1119
01:09:05,120 --> 01:09:11,160
are roughly at that highest of the crowd level.

1120
01:09:11,160 --> 01:09:12,480
Canada is down there.

1121
01:09:12,480 --> 01:09:14,040
The UK is a little lower.

1122
01:09:14,040 --> 01:09:15,760
Spain is a little lower.

1123
01:09:15,760 --> 01:09:17,160
Israel is a little lower.

1124
01:09:17,160 --> 01:09:20,600
Turkey is the lowest of the OECD countries

1125
01:09:20,600 --> 01:09:24,960
in terms of percentage of GDP that they spend on health care.

1126
01:09:24,960 --> 01:09:28,240
So, well, that's OK, but maybe we're

1127
01:09:28,240 --> 01:09:32,240
getting more for our money than these other countries.

1128
01:09:32,240 --> 01:09:34,600
And so there are a lot of analyses

1129
01:09:34,600 --> 01:09:36,160
that look at stuff like this.

1130
01:09:36,160 --> 01:09:43,680
They say, well, if we spend so much money per patient per year,

1131
01:09:43,680 --> 01:09:46,080
what do we get in terms of the simplest thing

1132
01:09:46,080 --> 01:09:48,720
to measure, which is life expectancy?

1133
01:09:48,720 --> 01:09:50,840
How long do people live?

1134
01:09:50,840 --> 01:09:53,960
And what we discover is that in the United States,

1135
01:09:53,960 --> 01:09:59,400
we're spending $9,000 a year on patients,

1136
01:09:59,400 --> 01:10:05,160
and we're getting a life span of somewhere in the high 70s.

1137
01:10:05,160 --> 01:10:08,280
So this is 2015 data.

1138
01:10:08,280 --> 01:10:11,800
Whereas in Switzerland, they're spending about $6,000,

1139
01:10:11,800 --> 01:10:17,800
so about 2 thirds, and they're getting 83 years or something

1140
01:10:17,800 --> 01:10:22,360
of life for the same price, and so on

1141
01:10:22,360 --> 01:10:24,640
for all these different countries.

1142
01:10:24,640 --> 01:10:28,160
By the way, this is all from GapMinder, which

1143
01:10:28,160 --> 01:10:31,000
is a wonderful data visualization tool.

1144
01:10:31,000 --> 01:10:32,440
And I don't have time to show you,

1145
01:10:32,440 --> 01:10:35,360
but you can click on individual lines there

1146
01:10:35,360 --> 01:10:40,320
and slide the slider about what era you're talking about.

1147
01:10:40,320 --> 01:10:42,800
And the data moves, and it's beautiful,

1148
01:10:42,800 --> 01:10:46,560
and it's a wonderful way of understanding it.

1149
01:10:46,560 --> 01:10:52,280
So there's an important thing to remember,

1150
01:10:52,280 --> 01:10:54,360
which was taught to me by my friend

1151
01:10:54,360 --> 01:10:57,440
Kristini at the Harvard Education School,

1152
01:10:57,440 --> 01:11:00,520
and that is that it's not even good enough to stand still.

1153
01:11:00,520 --> 01:11:04,640
So he suggested the following scenario.

1154
01:11:04,640 --> 01:11:07,360
If you look at the growth of productivity

1155
01:11:07,360 --> 01:11:12,280
over this period of 10 years by industry,

1156
01:11:12,280 --> 01:11:16,520
you discover that productivity went up by 7. something

1157
01:11:16,520 --> 01:11:20,720
percent for durable goods and went down by something

1158
01:11:20,720 --> 01:11:26,240
like 2% for mining, about 1% for construction.

1159
01:11:26,240 --> 01:11:31,480
Information technologies grew by 5.5% or something.

1160
01:11:31,480 --> 01:11:34,440
So if you ask the question, what happens

1161
01:11:34,440 --> 01:11:38,480
if the demand for these goods remains constant

1162
01:11:38,480 --> 01:11:41,040
over a period of time?

1163
01:11:41,040 --> 01:11:45,720
And what you discover is that because the more productive

1164
01:11:45,720 --> 01:11:49,280
things become cheaper, they wind up

1165
01:11:49,280 --> 01:11:52,600
occupying a smaller fraction of the total amount of money

1166
01:11:52,600 --> 01:11:55,240
that's being spent.

1167
01:11:55,240 --> 01:11:59,480
So your computer, your laptop, is a lot cheaper today

1168
01:11:59,480 --> 01:12:02,080
than it was 30 years ago.

1169
01:12:02,080 --> 01:12:05,240
And so that means that the amount of spending

1170
01:12:05,240 --> 01:12:10,920
that people do on things like information technology,

1171
01:12:10,920 --> 01:12:14,880
at least per item, is much lower than it used to be.

1172
01:12:14,880 --> 01:12:18,160
And if in the aggregate it's also lower than it used to be,

1173
01:12:18,160 --> 01:12:22,000
that means that something else must be higher

1174
01:12:22,000 --> 01:12:24,520
because it sums to 100%.

1175
01:12:24,520 --> 01:12:29,000
And so what this shows is that if you spend 30 years

1176
01:12:29,000 --> 01:12:31,840
at the same rates of productivity growth,

1177
01:12:31,880 --> 01:12:37,120
mining grows from whatever fraction of the economy

1178
01:12:37,120 --> 01:12:40,560
it was to something that's about three times as big

1179
01:12:40,560 --> 01:12:42,560
a fraction.

1180
01:12:42,560 --> 01:12:44,480
Right?

1181
01:12:44,480 --> 01:12:48,320
And if productivity growth is better,

1182
01:12:48,320 --> 01:12:52,840
then that sector of the economy shrinks.

1183
01:12:52,840 --> 01:12:57,520
So I think something like this is happening in health care,

1184
01:12:57,520 --> 01:13:00,280
which is that there's infinite demand.

1185
01:13:00,280 --> 01:13:02,960
And health care is not terribly productive.

1186
01:13:02,960 --> 01:13:06,240
We're not getting better as fast as electronics

1187
01:13:06,240 --> 01:13:09,920
is getting better, for example.

1188
01:13:09,920 --> 01:13:13,280
So people have tried doing various things.

1189
01:13:13,280 --> 01:13:18,280
Managed care was the buzzword of the 1980s and 90s.

1190
01:13:18,280 --> 01:13:20,160
And they said, well, what we're going to do

1191
01:13:20,160 --> 01:13:24,160
is to prevent people from overusing medical services

1192
01:13:24,160 --> 01:13:26,840
by requiring pre-admission review,

1193
01:13:26,840 --> 01:13:31,360
continued stay review, second surgical opinion.

1194
01:13:31,360 --> 01:13:34,280
We're going to have post-care management where

1195
01:13:34,280 --> 01:13:36,200
if you're released from the hospital

1196
01:13:36,200 --> 01:13:38,920
and you're in that second of the cycles,

1197
01:13:38,920 --> 01:13:41,680
people will call you at home and try to help you out

1198
01:13:41,680 --> 01:13:44,520
and make sure that you're doing whatever is best

1199
01:13:44,520 --> 01:13:47,000
to keep you out of the hospital.

1200
01:13:47,000 --> 01:13:49,920
And we're going to try various experiments

1201
01:13:49,920 --> 01:13:53,160
like institutional arrangements that say, well,

1202
01:13:53,160 --> 01:13:59,040
if I as a doctor agree to refer all my patients

1203
01:13:59,040 --> 01:14:03,240
to Mass General rather than to the Brigham or the BI,

1204
01:14:03,240 --> 01:14:05,400
they'll pay me extra.

1205
01:14:05,400 --> 01:14:10,320
And they'll get some kind of efficiencies from aggregation.

1206
01:14:10,320 --> 01:14:14,040
And so maybe that's one way of controlling costs.

1207
01:14:14,040 --> 01:14:18,000
So leakage is the idea of keeping people in the system.

1208
01:14:18,000 --> 01:14:20,280
And capitation is an interesting idea

1209
01:14:20,280 --> 01:14:25,320
which says that instead of paying for what the hospital does

1210
01:14:25,320 --> 01:14:27,880
for me or what the doctor does for me,

1211
01:14:27,880 --> 01:14:34,320
we simply pay him a flat fee for the year to take care of me.

1212
01:14:34,320 --> 01:14:36,520
And that takes away the incentive for him

1213
01:14:36,520 --> 01:14:39,640
to do more and more and more to get paid more.

1214
01:14:39,640 --> 01:14:41,320
But of course, it gives him an incentive

1215
01:14:41,320 --> 01:14:44,960
to do less and less and less so that he doesn't

1216
01:14:44,960 --> 01:14:46,320
have to spend as much money.

1217
01:14:46,320 --> 01:14:48,480
And so it's sort of a knife balance

1218
01:14:48,480 --> 01:14:50,280
to figure out how to do this.

1219
01:14:50,280 --> 01:14:53,200
But that's an important component.

1220
01:14:53,200 --> 01:14:56,160
So if you look at the evaluation of managed care

1221
01:14:56,160 --> 01:14:59,200
a long time ago, what they said was,

1222
01:14:59,200 --> 01:15:05,160
it helped reduce inpatient costs by increasing outpatient costs.

1223
01:15:05,160 --> 01:15:07,240
So what it's done is it's pushed people

1224
01:15:07,240 --> 01:15:11,280
from going to the hospital into going to their doctor's office.

1225
01:15:11,280 --> 01:15:14,680
But it's been pretty much a wash in terms of overall spending.

1226
01:15:15,360 --> 01:15:20,480
But doctors also hated managed care.

1227
01:15:20,480 --> 01:15:24,760
I was sitting with one of my colleagues at a Boston hospital

1228
01:15:24,760 --> 01:15:27,960
and an insurance company clerk called him

1229
01:15:27,960 --> 01:15:34,000
to done him for having ordered a certain test on a heart patient.

1230
01:15:34,000 --> 01:15:36,520
And so he was furious.

1231
01:15:36,520 --> 01:15:39,640
And he turns to her and says, so which medical school

1232
01:15:39,640 --> 01:15:41,720
do you have your diploma from?

1233
01:15:41,720 --> 01:15:44,320
And of course, she doesn't have a medical degree.

1234
01:15:44,360 --> 01:15:47,040
She's following some rules on a sheet

1235
01:15:47,040 --> 01:15:51,720
about how to harass doctors, not to order expensive tests.

1236
01:15:51,720 --> 01:15:55,920
And so we have Edward Anas, the past president of the AMA,

1237
01:15:55,920 --> 01:15:58,560
who says, well, in the glory days,

1238
01:15:58,560 --> 01:16:01,760
there was no bureaucratic regimentation, no forms,

1239
01:16:01,760 --> 01:16:03,360
no blah, blah, blah.

1240
01:16:03,360 --> 01:16:05,960
And all my patients were happy, and I was happy,

1241
01:16:05,960 --> 01:16:08,360
and things were ideal.

1242
01:16:08,360 --> 01:16:10,880
If you actually look back at those days,

1243
01:16:10,920 --> 01:16:15,040
it wasn't as good as he cracks it up to be.

1244
01:16:15,040 --> 01:16:17,240
And some of those issues of disparity

1245
01:16:17,240 --> 01:16:19,720
that we were talking about were horrible.

1246
01:16:19,720 --> 01:16:22,160
So for his patients who were rich

1247
01:16:22,160 --> 01:16:26,520
and who could afford to see him, life was pretty good,

1248
01:16:26,520 --> 01:16:31,840
but not so much for underserved populations.

1249
01:16:31,840 --> 01:16:36,600
So you've seen Obamacare come and partly go

1250
01:16:36,600 --> 01:16:39,680
and continues to be controversial.

1251
01:16:39,680 --> 01:16:46,440
But it's trying to foster better information technology

1252
01:16:46,440 --> 01:16:51,000
as a basis for getting doctors to make better decisions.

1253
01:16:51,000 --> 01:16:53,520
It's trying to foster these accountable care

1254
01:16:53,520 --> 01:16:56,840
organizations, which is a version of capitation,

1255
01:16:56,840 --> 01:17:00,840
to put the pressure on to reduce the amount of health services

1256
01:17:00,840 --> 01:17:03,400
that people are asking for.

1257
01:17:03,400 --> 01:17:07,200
There is a hospital readmission reduction program

1258
01:17:07,200 --> 01:17:14,040
now that says, if you are a Medicare patient, for example,

1259
01:17:14,040 --> 01:17:16,240
and you're discharged from the hospital

1260
01:17:16,240 --> 01:17:21,120
and you're readmitted within 30 days of your discharge,

1261
01:17:21,120 --> 01:17:23,480
then they're going to don you and not

1262
01:17:23,480 --> 01:17:25,640
pay you for that readmission or not

1263
01:17:25,640 --> 01:17:27,920
pay you for part of that readmission.

1264
01:17:27,920 --> 01:17:31,560
But if you look at the statistics, which I just did,

1265
01:17:31,560 --> 01:17:35,440
that's the distribution of the payment adjustment factors.

1266
01:17:35,440 --> 01:17:39,200
So it turns out the lowest number is 97%.

1267
01:17:39,200 --> 01:17:42,400
So a 3% decrease in reimbursement is something

1268
01:17:42,400 --> 01:17:45,880
that the CFO of your hospital would really care about,

1269
01:17:45,880 --> 01:17:50,320
but it's not like a 25% reduction in reimbursement.

1270
01:17:50,320 --> 01:17:53,480
So this has had a fairly minor effect.

1271
01:17:56,800 --> 01:18:01,800
Let me just finish by saying that money determines much.

1272
01:18:01,800 --> 01:18:05,240
From our point of view, one of the problems we face

1273
01:18:05,240 --> 01:18:09,440
is that IT traditionally gets about 1% to 2%

1274
01:18:09,440 --> 01:18:13,760
of spending in medical centers, whereas it gets about 6%

1275
01:18:13,760 --> 01:18:19,880
or 7% in business overall and about 10% to 12% for banking.

1276
01:18:19,880 --> 01:18:24,280
And a lot of these systems are managed by accountants,

1277
01:18:24,280 --> 01:18:26,440
although that is slowly changing.

1278
01:18:26,440 --> 01:18:31,040
So in the 1990s, HST, with Harvard

1279
01:18:31,040 --> 01:18:35,400
started a training program for medical doctors

1280
01:18:35,400 --> 01:18:37,640
to become medical informaticians.

1281
01:18:37,640 --> 01:18:40,960
So practicing this sort of witchcraft.

1282
01:18:40,960 --> 01:18:43,840
And our first two graduates, one of them

1283
01:18:43,840 --> 01:18:47,320
is now CIO at the Beth Israel Deaconess.

1284
01:18:47,320 --> 01:18:50,320
The second one is CIO at Children's Hospital.

1285
01:18:50,320 --> 01:18:53,560
And so one of my big successes personally

1286
01:18:53,560 --> 01:18:56,200
has been to displace some accountants

1287
01:18:56,200 --> 01:18:59,480
by doctors who actually understand this technology

1288
01:18:59,520 --> 01:19:01,560
to some extent.

1289
01:19:01,560 --> 01:19:05,160
OK, I think that's all I'm going to say.

1290
01:19:05,160 --> 01:19:08,800
There's a funny last slide here, which

1291
01:19:08,800 --> 01:19:12,320
has a pointer that I would want you to remember.

1292
01:19:12,320 --> 01:19:15,560
The slides will be up on our website.

1293
01:19:15,560 --> 01:19:17,320
You can follow it.

1294
01:19:17,320 --> 01:19:19,440
MIT has a program called GEMS.

1295
01:19:19,440 --> 01:19:23,240
It's the general education in medical sciences

1296
01:19:23,240 --> 01:19:27,200
intended as a minor program for people in the PhD program

1297
01:19:27,200 --> 01:19:29,080
in some other field.

1298
01:19:29,080 --> 01:19:33,160
And if you're serious about really concentrating

1299
01:19:33,160 --> 01:19:37,640
on health care, developing at least the kind of understanding

1300
01:19:37,640 --> 01:19:41,120
of the health care process that I've tried to give you today

1301
01:19:41,120 --> 01:19:44,720
and that would allow you to play a doctor on television

1302
01:19:44,720 --> 01:19:46,360
is really important.

1303
01:19:46,360 --> 01:19:48,280
And there is a program that helps

1304
01:19:48,280 --> 01:19:51,160
you achieve that, which I commend to people.

1305
01:19:51,160 --> 01:19:54,080
OK, see you next week.

1306
01:19:59,080 --> 01:20:01,080
Thank you.

